<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wihtred of Kent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 725</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wihtred was king of Kent from about 690 to 725, restoring stability after a period of invasion and disputed succession. His law code of 695 deals mainly with the Church, setting penalties for pagan worship, unlawful marriage and failure to keep the Sabbath, and he granted the Kentish churches freedom from taxation. His long reign was a time of peace.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wihtred was a son of King Ecgberht I of Kent and a brother of Eadric. When Ecgberht died in 673 his sons were probably only two or three years old. Their uncle Hlothhere became king in 674, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfhere-of-mercia">Wulfhere of Mercia</a>, the boys' uncle by marriage, may have ruled Kent in the year between. Eadric rose against Hlothhere, who died of his wounds in 685 or 686, and after Eadric's own death the kingdom fell into disorder. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Cædwalla of Wessex</a> invaded in 686 and made his brother Mul king; later Oswine and Swæfheard, son of King Sebbi of Essex, reigned at the same time in east and west Kent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wihtred emerged from this disarray in the early 690s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> called him the rightful king who freed the nation from foreign invasion, a view that probably came from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> informant, Abbot Albinus, and has been called strongly partisan, since Oswine was also of the royal family. Two charters point to an accession between April and July 691, while Bede's figure of thirty-four and a half years implies late 690. He at first ruled with Swæfheard, who is not heard of after July 692, and by 694 he was sole king.</p><p>That year he made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a>, agreeing compensation for the killing of Mul, whom the Kentishmen had burned; most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> give the sum as thirty thousand. He probably also ceded some border land. He is thought to have had three wives, Cynegyth, Æthelburh and Wærburh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His laws, preserved in the Textus Roffensis, were probably issued on 6 September 695 at Berghamstyde, most likely Bearsted near Maidstone, with Archbishop Bertwald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and Bishop Gefmund of Rochester present. Only the last four of the twenty-eight chapters are not about the Church. A bishop's oath was made incontrovertible, equal to the king's, and clause 21 let a ceorl clear himself with three oath-helpers of his own class. A shared clause and the West Saxon word gesith suggest that Wihtred and Ine worked together on their codes. He died on 23 April 725 and left Kent to his sons Æthelberht II, Eadberht I and Alric.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wihtred of Kent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kent was rich from trade with Francia, producing fine garnet jewellery and gold coinage, and later kings such as <b>Wihtred</b> issued further laws.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a></li></ul></section>
